A: You need to test the .NET framework thoroughly, including boundary values. Possible test cases might include an empty list, a list with one item, a list with many items, duplicate items, and items at the exact boundaries of an array's index. 

As for the specific method being the one under test (LastIndexOf), it could be tested in numerous ways. Consider testing different types of lists (ordered, unordered, large, small), items that are in the list, items that are not in the list, and check what happens if a null item is passed as argument. 

Also note that the LastIndexOf method could return two different values based on whether the item is found once or multiple times within the list. 

Regardless, creating your own custom "AssumeUnderTest" method won't allow the Pex to work as it doesn't handle setup/teardown methods, it can only handle public methods of the target class. 

So while running, make sure the class name and method in the [TestMethod] attribute matches exactly the PexGeneratedTest method signature. Pex will not execute the AssumeUnderTest because it is a helper method. It is used by the framework to test private methods.

Note: A method marked as private/internal can still be tested because Pex can't reorder methods marked as private or internal. 

Also, remember that private or internal methods could still be unit tested separately outside of these helper methods. It's just not as straightforward to test all interactions between your private/internal methods in one large unit test.

Regarding your question about writing test cases and code, here is a general structure:

[TestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void YourTestMethod([PexAssumeUnderTest]YourClassUnderTest instance, string parameter)
{
  //Arrange
  //Act
  var result = instance.YourMethodUnderTest(parameter);
  //Assert
  //Your assertions here
}

Note that PexMethod attribute is used to enable Pex to execute the test. And [PexAssumeUnderTest] is used to tell Pex that you want it--------------------            
            Assert.IsTrue(x < 0 || (x >= 0 && New_arrListContainsX), "The last index of value {3} was expected to be positive", Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, x, New_Ret);

            Assert.IsTrue(x >= 0 && New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX, "The last index of value {3} was expected to be {4}", Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, x, New_Ret, Old_arrListIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}
```
该方法根据约定检查指定的元素的最后出现的索引。

该方法的假设条件确保输入数组列表不为空，并且至少包含一个元素，否则`IndexOf`和`Contains`方法可能存在问题。

观察了几个值，包括旧数组列表和新数组列表的元素计数，索引，LastIndexOf的结果(New_Ret)，指定元素是否存在于列表中（`arrList.Contains(x)`），以及该方法的预期行为是否符合约定（如果x为负，则指定的元素不存在于列表中）。

最后，使用Debug.Assert检查LastIndexOf结果是否符合预期。Assert.IsTrue方法的第一个参数指定要在失败时显示的消息，其余的参数提供有关条件及其不满足的上下文的更多信息。PexObserve允许您在Visual Studio中查看这些值。

这是一个很好的基本示例，演示了Pex-style的约定测试，它验证了方法（在这种情况下是ArrayList的LastIndexOf方法）的行为是否符合指定的要求，并提供了有关其行为的更多背景信息。

