            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

            PexPostcondition.IsTrue(true);

        }
    }
}
```
# PexTests in C\#: Testing Contains Method

This is an example of a PexTest that verifies the functionality of the Contains method in List collections in C#. The method to be tested is the Contains method of the ArrayList Class.

Here's your example of a PexTest that verifies the Contains method in C#:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using NUnit.Framework;
using System.Collections.ArrayList;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{   
    [TestFixture]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        //Testing Contains method of ArrayList
        [PexMethod]
        [PexAssemblyToTargetAssembly("netstandard.dll")]
        public void ArrayListContractTest_Contains([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList al, [PexPositiveValue(int.MaxValue)]int item)
        {
            //Assume the collection is not empty
            Assume.That(al.Count != 0);
            
            //Execute
            bool exists=al.Contains(item);

            //Ensure element exists in the collection
            Assert.IsTrue(exists, "The item does not exist in the arraylist");
            
            //Postcondition: The exists variable should be true
            PexPostcondition.IsTrue(exists);
        }        
    }
}
```
In the above code, given an ArrayList and a positive integer, we assume that the ArrayList is not empty. 

Then we execute a call to Contains method. In the end, if the method returns false, the Assert will fail indicating that the item is not present in the list.

The PexPostcondition.IsTrue(exists) ensures that after a call to Contains has been made the returned value is indeed true i.e. the item exists in the list, ensuring our Contains function is correct.

NUnit framework is used here for assertions and NUnit also supports the Pex framework which helps to generate a lot of test cases automatically when we specify the parameters.

This is a simple PexTest verification of ArrayList's Contains method and it provides a starting point for you to create more complex tests on your own. Please modify or add more Pex methods as per your requirements. 

In a real-world scenario, ArrayList is replaced with your specific data structure and the 'PexAssemblyToTargetAssembly' attribute is used to point Pex to the relevant NET Standard DLL. Make sure your project has a reference to the correct .NET Standard version.
  
Please note that the Pex is a commercial tool from Microsoft, which comes with a cost and should be used at the discretion of the team or organization to ensure it's used properly and effectively within the project. It can help to ensure the correctness of code because it generates a bunch of test cases based on the method signatures and arguments, but it does not guarantee test coverage. It's a good tool for ensuring the basic functionality of the code without relying on the coverage metrics. The Pex tool analyzes the method under test and generates a set of test inputs covering edge cases and other scenarios. The generated code is then run and checked against expectations to ensure that the method behaves as expected. 

However, if the complexity of tests increases and a method under test starts becoming more unpredictable or difficult to manage (like a complex algorithm), it would be reasonable to consider other tools like code coverage tools, behavior-driven development (BDD) or specification-by-example (SBE) frameworks from third-party developers or from Microsoft itself such as NUnit, Moq, and MSTest and xUnit that provide more control, flexibility and detailed test cases rather than just the automatic creation of test cases like Pex. 
  
The Pex tool is mainly designed for "Quick Check"--------------------		// If the return value is non-negative and the array list does contain x, then the value should be less than or equal to old array list index of x
        }

        [PexMethodUnderTest(typeof(ArrayList))]
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContractTestData([PexChoose(typeof(int), 0, 1)] int x, [PexChoose(typeof(ArrayList), null, new int[]{1,2,3,4})] ArrayList arrList)
        {
            PUT_LastIndexOfContract(arrList, x);
        }
    }
}
```

このコントラクトでは、ArrayListの最終的なLastIndexOfメソッド実行後に、一連のアサーションにより一連の条件をチェックしている。なお、Pexは、こうしたアサーションを使用して、入力の不一致の有無にかかわらず、メソッド実行前後の要素数や要素の存在などを比較できる。詳しくはPexの公式ページを参照の上、下記の参考リンクもご参照ください。

